From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 07:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] kv-cache: before a batch whose span would pass the VRAM line, move its cells low

0038 and 0039 keep a batch's span to its own cells, but cells already
placed stay where they are: a conversation prefilled while an idle one
held the bottom of the pool lives above it for good (pagoda-h6: the new
conversation's 67k cells at 176k+, all past the 114,432-cell VRAM line of
the tiered cache (0028), staged over PCIe on every step even after the old
one was cleared).

At the one moment a move pays -- the memory update before a batch whose
span would reach past the VRAM line -- the cells of the batch's sequences
move to the lowest cells of the pool, and cells of sequences no recent
batch used move out of the way:

- llama_context::decode tells the memory the batch's sequences, its token
  count and n_ubatch (llama_memory_i::set_next_batch; hybrid: its
  attention cache) before memory_update().
- init_update plans (plan_moves). S = the batch's sequences; P = sequences
  in one of the last n_seq_max batches but not this one (they are running
  too: a draft context decodes each slot on its own), never moved. Nothing
  happens unless (a) the batch's span -- its highest cell, or its new cells
  placed from the lowest free cell -- passes the VRAM line, and (b) it is
  at least a block above every S and P cell plus room for
  max(n_tokens, n_ubatch) new cells (per-sequence counts and spans, no
  scan). Below the line nothing moves: a move there saves only masked VRAM
  reads and changes the order the attention op reads the cells in, and
  with it the rounding of its sums. Then one pass over the cells: R = the
  lowest K cells that are not P's, K = S's cells + that room; S cells above
  R go to empty cells in R, else exchange with an idle cell in R (which
  lands where the S cell was); the idle cells left in R move to the lowest
  empty cells above R -- just past the new span, never parked at the top of
  the pool. The room makes this once per ubatch of new cells.
- update() applies it after any K-shift (apply_moves): per chunk of at most
  n_ubatch rows (a gathered chunk is no larger than one F32 activation of a
  ubatch's graph, which the reserved compute buffer holds), one graph of
  ggml_get_rows + ggml_set_rows per K and V tensor over a raw alias of the
  tensor as 32-bit words -- the bytes move, never a value; every source row
  is gathered before any destination is written, so exchanges need no
  scratch. The cells' state (swap_cells) follows each graph. Host-backed
  rows of the tiered buffer are read and written through its VMM mapping.

Tiered caches only (--kv-vram-cells), and needs whole rows per cell: one
stream, no transposed V (flash attention), no SWA, rows a multiple of 4
bytes, no shared cells; otherwise off. One INFO line per move
("kv promote: ..."). LLAMA_KV_PROMOTE=0 turns it off;
LLAMA_KV_PROMOTE_VERIFY=1 reads a sample of moved rows before and after and
logs whether they are byte-identical. Measurements: docs/ENGINES.md
"KV placement" (bench/kv_placement.py).
